<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1527 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Chad Gadya</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="arc" dir="rtl">חד גדיא</bdi> / <bdi lang="arc-Latn">Chad Gadya</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A cumulative counting-on song about one little goat, sung by families at the end of the Passover meal.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jewish communities; the Passover Seder</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Provence, France; Prague, Czech Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>early version c. 13th–14th century; printed in Haggadahs in Prague, 1527 and 1590</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional (early version 13th–14th century; printed in Prague in 1527))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad-gadya">Chad Gadya</a> (&quot;one little goat&quot; or &quot;one kid&quot;) is a playful cumulative song sung at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> Seder, the ritual feast that opens the Jewish holiday of Passover. An early version appears in a prayer book of the Jewish community of Provence from about the 13th or 14th century, and the song was printed in Haggadahs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1527 and 1590, which makes it a late addition to the Seder. Its melody may have roots in medieval German folk music. It is popular with children, like the Haggadah's other cumulative song, Echad Mi Yodea (&quot;Who Knows One?&quot;).</p><p>Although it is often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, the song mixes Aramaic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, and its composer's grasp of Aramaic seems to have been limited: it opens with Aramaic words for the goat, the cat and the dog, but towards the end uses Hebrew for the slaughterer, the Angel of Death and God, and its grammar slips between masculine and feminine. Nor was Aramaic used to hide its meaning from non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, since its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> included a full German translation. It is also sung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>, Ladino, Judaeo-Italian and Judaeo-Arabic, and Haggadahs differ over whether the father bought or sold the goat.</p><p>Behind the light-hearted song commentators have found deeper meanings. A popular reading, apparently first widely published in a pamphlet by Philip Nicodemus Lebrecht in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1731, makes it an allegory of the nations that conquered the Land of Israel, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, with the goat as the Jewish people and God bringing them home at the end; many variants assign the oppressors differently. Rabbi Jacob <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a> read it in 1795 as the perils facing the soul in life; Rabbi Jonathan Eybeschuetz as a short history of Israel from the covenant with Abraham to the Roman conqueror Titus; Rabbi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> Sofer as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> rite in the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilna-gaon">Vilna Gaon</a> as a history from the birthright of Abraham and Isaac to the Messiah. These readings were summarised by Rabbi Kenneth Brander in the Journal of Jewish Music &amp; Liturgy in 1994.</p><p>In 1917 and 1919 the Russian avant-garde artist El Lissitzky made two illustrated books of Had Gadya, with the verses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>. Art historians have read the change between them, in which the Angel of Death is cast down in the first and plainly dead in the second, with his victims raised to life, as showing his sympathy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> and the end of discrimination against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. In 1989 an anti-war version by the Israeli singer Chava Alberstein drew calls for a ban on Israeli radio; it became well known and is now often played at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>. The song gave its title to Wolf Mankowitz's 1953 novel A Kid for Two Farthings, became a popular song in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as Angelo Branduardi's &quot;Alla fiera dell'est&quot; in the 1970s, and appears in Henry Roth's novel Call It Sleep (1934) and many films and television series. In Yiddish slang &quot;chad gadya&quot; also means jail.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A cumulative chain: a father buys a little goat for two small coins, and each verse brings on something that overcomes the one before, from a cat and a dog through a stick, fire, water, an ox, a slaughterer and the Angel of Death, and at last God. Each verse repeats the whole chain back to the goat, so the song grows with every verse; it is cheerful in tone, though often read as an allegory of suffering and redemption.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Aramaic</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Aramaic</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">khad gadya, khad gadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One kid, one kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא שׁוּנְרָא, וְאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata shunra, veakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came a cat and ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא כַלְבָּא, וְנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata khalba, venashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came a dog and bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא חוּטְרָא, וְהִכָּה לְכַלְבָּא, דְּנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata khutra, vehikkah lekhalba, denashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came a stick and beat the dog, that bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא נוּרָא, וְשָׂרַף לְחוּטְרָא, דְּהִכָּה לְכַלְבָּא, דְּנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata nura, vesaraf lekhutra, dehikkah lekhalba, denashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came a fire and burned the stick, that beat the dog, that bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא מַיָּא, וְכָבָה לְנוּרָא, דְּשָׂרַף לְחוּטְרָא, דְּהִכָּה לְכַלְבָּא, דְּנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata mayya, vekhavah lenura, desaraf lekhutra, dehikkah lekhalba, denashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came water and put out the fire, that burned the stick, that beat the dog, that bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא תוֹרָא, וְשָׁתָה לְמַיָּא, דְּכָבָה לְנוּרָא, דְּשָׂרַף לְחוּטְרָא, דְּהִכָּה לְכַלְבָּא, דְּנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata tora, veshatah lemayya, dekhavah lenura, desaraf lekhutra, dehikkah lekhalba, denashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came an ox and drank the water, that put out the fire, that burned the stick, that beat the dog, that bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא הַשּׁוֹחֵט, וְשָׁחַט לְתוֹרָא, דְּשָׁתָה לְמַיָּא, דְּכָבָה לְנוּרָא, דְּשָׂרַף לְחוּטְרָא, דְּהִכָּה לְכַלְבָּא, דְּנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata hashshokhet, veshakhat letora, deshatah lemayya, dekhavah lenura, desaraf lekhutra, dehikkah lekhalba, denashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came the slaughterer and slaughtered the ox, that drank the water, that put out the fire, that burned the stick, that beat the dog, that bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא מַלְאַךְ הַמָּֽוֶת, וְשָׁחַט לְשׁוֹחֵט, דְּשָׁחַט לְתוֹרָא, דְּשָׁתָה לְמַיָּא, דְּכָבָה לְנוּרָא, דְּשָׂרַף לְחוּטְרָא, דְּהִכָּה לְכַלְבָּא, דְּנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata malakh hammavet, veshakhat leshokhet, deshakhat letora, deshatah lemayya, dekhavah lenura, desaraf lekhutra, dehikkah lekhalba, denashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came the Angel of Death and slaughtered the slaughterer, that slaughtered the ox, that drank the water, that put out the fire, that burned the stick, that beat the dog, that bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="arc" dir="rtl">וַאֲתָא הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא, וְשָׁחַט לְמַלְאָךְ הַמָּֽוֶת, דְּשָׁחַט לְשׁוֹחֵט, דְּשָׁחַט לְתוֹרָא, דְּשָׁתָה לְמַיָּא, דְּכָבָה לְנוּרָא, דְּשָׂרַף לְחוּטְרָא, דְּהִכָּה לְכַלְבָּא, דְּנָשַׁךְ לְשׁוּנְרָא, דְּאָכְלָה לְגַדְיָא, דְּזַבִּין אַבָּא בִּתְרֵי זוּזֵי, חַד גַּדְיָא, חַד גַּדְיָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="arc-Latn">vaata haqqadosh barukh hu, veshakhat lemalakh hammavet, deshakhat leshokhet, deshakhat letora, deshatah lemayya, dekhavah lenura, desaraf lekhutra, dehikkah lekhalba, denashakh leshunra, deakhlah legadya, dezabbin abba bitre zuze, khad gadya, khad gadya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then came the Holy One, blessed be he, and slaughtered the Angel of Death, that slaughtered the slaughterer, that slaughtered the ox, that drank the water, that put out the fire, that burned the stick, that beat the dog, that bit the cat, that ate the kid, that father bought for two zuzim: one kid, one kid.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; the Aramaic text of the Passover Haggadah, as it ends the Seder. Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%97%D7%93_%D7%92%D7%93%D7%99%D7%90" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose (Aramaic)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>'abba' is 'father' (often taken as 'my father'); a zuz (plural zuzim) was a small silver coin.</li><li>'the Holy One, blessed be he' (haqqadosh barukh hu) is the rabbinic name of God; the phrase is Hebrew within the Aramaic.</li><li>stanza 1: the opening of each stanza after it, 'vaata', is 'and there came'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
